---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import Diagram from '@/components/Diagram.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="Meetings, feedback &amp; decision records"
  description="How OPDA combines meetings, asynchronous discussion, public review and durable issue records without treating message volume or attendance as authority."
>
      <h1>Meetings, feedback &amp; decision records</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Meetings help people understand and challenge the work. Persistent channels make
        participation possible between meetings. A durable issue-and-disposition register—not a
        meeting, chat thread or reaction count—preserves the standards decision record.
      </p>

      <div class="callout callout--warn">
        <div class="callout__label">Status — ratified facts, evidenced practice and proposals</div>
        <p>
          The AGM and Board requirements and the 10-year Board/Executive Committee decision-record
          baseline come from OPDA's governing documents. Archived meeting cadences are evidenced
          practice, not formally codified rules. The standards feedback, consensus, public-review
          and record-keeping model comes from <a href="/modelling/adr/adr-0068">proposed
          ADR-0068</a> and is not yet operative.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="principles">Five operating principles</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Questions are welcome during meetings.</strong> A live session is for orientation, scrutiny and discussion—not automatic approval.</li>
        <li><strong>Decisions remain open asynchronously.</strong> A proposal first discussed in a meeting receives at least five working days for confirmation under ADR-0068.</li>
        <li><strong>One issue, one durable record.</strong> Related discussion from Teams, the website, meetings and email is linked to the same issue.</li>
        <li><strong>Reply in threads.</strong> Persistent channels should keep one subject per post and subsequent discussion beneath it, so participation can scale.</li>
        <li><strong>Participation is not authority.</strong> Attendance, message volume, reactions and AI agreement do not establish consensus.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="topology">Where discussion and decisions happen</h2>
      <Diagram size="large" caption="Discussion may begin anywhere, but substantive standards work is routed through one issue record to the body with the relevant authority. Stage decisions and release manifests are maintained separately by the proposed Standards Secretariat.">
flowchart LR
  M["`**Meetings**
  orientation · challenge`"]:::infra
  T["`**Teams**
  persistent threaded discussion`"]:::infra
  W["`**Website review**
  exact candidate · public feedback`"]:::infra
  E["`**Email / GitHub / evidence**
  other inputs`"]:::infra
  I["`**Canonical issue record**
  source · affiliation · impact
  discussion · disposition`"]:::data
  A{"`**Route by authority**`"}:::warning
  D["Domain Working Group
  *domain meaning*"]:::service
  X["Interoperability Working Group
  *boundaries + mappings*"]:::service
  G["Technical / risk gates
  *independent checks*"]:::process
  R["`**Stage + decision record**
  exact proposal · reasons · result`"]:::success

  M --> I
  T --> I
  W --> I
  E --> I
  I --> A
  A --> D
  A --> X
  A --> G
  D --> R
  X --> R
  G --> R
      </Diagram>

      <h2 id="channels">Channels and their purpose</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Surface</th><th>Use it for</th><th>Do not treat it as</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Teams channels</strong></td>
            <td>Working-group updates, questions, evidence requests and discussion of candidates. Use one clear topic per post and reply in its thread.</td>
            <td>The sole decision record or a vote by reaction count.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Meetings</strong></td>
            <td>Orientation, demonstrations, domain explanation, challenge and working through difficult issues.</td>
            <td>A venue where absence becomes consent or a live decision is instantly final.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Website</strong></td>
            <td>The exact candidate, definitions, diagrams, data dictionary, glossary, shapes, generated outputs, evidence citations, open questions and change diff.</td>
            <td>Proof that a candidate has advanced or become normative.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Public-review discussion</strong></td>
            <td>Comments on a clearly identified Public Review Draft during its published window.</td>
            <td>The canonical issue-and-disposition register.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Email and GitHub</strong></td>
            <td>Access problems, private procedural matters, technical reports and other inputs that need routing.</td>
            <td>A parallel, invisible decision path.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 id="cadence">Known cadence and proposed additions</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Body or activity</th><th>Cadence</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>General Assembly / AGM</strong></td><td>Annually, no later than 31 October</td><td>Ratified · Articles arts. 24–34</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Board of Directors</strong></td><td>At least four meetings each year</td><td>Ratified · Article 10</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>DPMSG Steering Group</strong></td><td>Monthly in the archived 2025–26 record</td><td>Evidenced practice—not formally codified</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Engagement Working Group update</strong></td><td>Monthly in the archived 2026 record</td><td>Evidenced practice—not formally codified</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Domain Working Groups</strong></td><td>Defined in each charter; asynchronous work continues between meetings</td><td>Proposed standards operating model</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Interoperability Working Group</strong></td><td>Defined in its charter and aligned to domain hand-offs</td><td>Proposed standards operating model</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Public review</strong></td><td>Event-driven by a candidate's maturity, not a fixed meeting cycle</td><td>Proposed in ADR-0068</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        Meeting frequency should be set by workload and participant capacity. A busy calendar is
        not evidence of good governance; clear decisions, timely dispositions and broad
        participation are.
      </p>

      <h2 id="meeting-record">What every standards meeting records</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>date, purpose, convenor, attendees, affiliations and declared interests;</li>
        <li>the agenda and exact artefact or issue versions discussed;</li>
        <li>evidence presented, material alternatives and unresolved questions;</li>
        <li>proposals to take to asynchronous review—not simply “agreed in the room”;</li>
        <li>actions, owners, due dates and links to canonical issues; and</li>
        <li>recording and transcript details where participants were notified and the use is lawful.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Recordings and transcripts are evidence inputs. They are not decisions, and they do not
        authorise AI to infer agreement from tone, attendance or silence.
      </p>

      <h2 id="issue-record">The canonical issue and disposition record</h2>
      <p>Every substantive question should contain:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>a stable ID, title, bounded context and affected artefact/version;</li>
        <li>proposer, affiliation, date, evidence links and rights classification;</li>
        <li>the problem, options, candidate change and interoperability/compatibility impact;</li>
        <li>links to relevant threads, meetings and reviewer comments;</li>
        <li>declared interests and disclosure of material AI assistance;</li>
        <li>a reasoned disposition: accepted, needs evidence, deferred, duplicate/out of scope, or not accepted;</li>
        <li>any dissent, Formal Objection, remedy, response or appeal; and</li>
        <li>the eligible decision set, result, Chair, date, implementation link and validation result.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout callout--note">
        <div class="callout__label">Tool-independent governance</div>
        <p>
          Teams, Artalk, GitHub or another interface may make discussion easier. None should be
          allowed to become the only copy of the formal record. The register must preserve stable
          identifiers, provenance, dispositions and decisions even if a discussion tool changes.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="consensus">Consensus calls</h2>
      <p>
        ADR-0068 proposes a default 10-working-day call identifying the exact proposal, diff,
        evidence, open risks and deadline. Silence is abstention. Unless a charter is stronger,
        advancement needs explicit support from at least three independent organisations across
        two materially affected stakeholder categories. Voting is an exceptional deadlock route,
        not the default way to settle technical meaning.
      </p>

      <h2 id="public-review">Public review</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Review event</th><th>Minimum proposed window</th><th>Required outcome</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>First Public Review Draft</strong></td>
            <td>30 calendar days</td>
            <td>Every material comment acknowledged, linked to an issue and given a reasoned disposition.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Material change after review</strong></td>
            <td>15 calendar days for the affected material</td>
            <td>The changed material is reviewed again before advancement.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Editorial correction</strong></td>
            <td>No restarted public-review window</td>
            <td>Independent review and a change-log entry.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Closing the review window does not authorise release. Comment disposition, consensus,
        independent technical and risk gates, conformance, implementation evidence and formal
        ratification still follow. See
        <a href="/governance/standards-lifecycle#public-review">standards lifecycle &amp; ratification</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="retention">Decision records and retention</h2>
      <p>
        Article 16 requires Executive Committee and Board decision records to be kept for at
        least 10 years. ADR-0068 proposes using that as the baseline for standards stage-decision
        records as well. The proposed Standards Secretariat maintains charters, rosters, notices,
        minutes, issue and disposition records, stage registers, review windows, release manifests
        and appeals; it verifies completeness but does not approve content.
      </p>

      <h2 id="measures">Useful operating measures</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>age of untriaged evidence and unresolved blocking issues;</li>
        <li>time to acknowledge and dispose substantive comments;</li>
        <li>participating organisations and affected stakeholder breadth;</li>
        <li>coverage, validation, conformance and implementation results;</li>
        <li>appeals, overdue reviews and decisions without complete records.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Raw message volume, meeting attendance, reaction counts and model agreement are not
        quality measures.
      </p>

      <h2 id="open-decisions">Operational decisions still required</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>the initial Standards Secretariat, accountable sponsor and named role holders;</li>
        <li>approved Domain and Interoperability Working Group charters and their cadences;</li>
        <li>the issue/stage register implementation and public consultation interface;</li>
        <li>service targets for triage and disposition that reflect volunteer capacity;</li>
        <li>recording, transcription, retention and access rules; and</li>
        <li>contribution, IPR and confidentiality terms before first public review.</li>
      </ul>

